WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.434
-Welcome. Welcome back.
So happy to see you in person.

00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:05.367
-I'm so happy to see -- You know
what I found out back there?

00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:06.667
It's my sixth time here.
-Yeah.

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:08.634
-Isn't that amazing?
-Does it feel like six times?

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:11.067
-I don't know. I just --
-We have fun every time.

00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:12.934
-Every time. Thank you.
I love being here.

00:00:12.934 --> 00:00:14.167
I love watching it.

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:16.467
And I got a lot of love
for all of you here.

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:18.901
-Oh, we love having you here.
-Lot of love.

00:00:18.901 --> 00:00:22.067
-I didn't get a chance to tell
you happy birthday.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:23.400
Happy 27th birthday, right?

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.634
Yeah, this is a big deal.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:26.634 --> 00:00:29.968
But here is what
I think is very funny.

00:00:29.968 --> 00:00:32.334
You celebrated your
21st birthday.

00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:33.701
-I did.

00:00:33.701 --> 00:00:37.000
-Now, why did it take you
so long to celebrate your 21st?

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
-I turned 21 when I was
shooting in Vancouver,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.634
and the legal drinking age
there is 18.

00:00:41.634 --> 00:00:43.934
So when I went around being
like, "It's my 21st!",

00:00:43.934 --> 00:00:45.300
people were like, "Okay."

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.334
And then, the next year --
-It's like, "Yeah, that's fine."

00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:49.133
-You know, I thought
maybe I'd celebrate it

00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:50.601
the next year and the next year,
and it never happened.

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:54.100
And, finally, this year,
post-booster, of course,

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.033
I celebrated my 21st birthday
six years later.

00:00:58.033 --> 00:00:59.067
-That's right.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:02.267
Why not?
-I know. And I did it up.

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:03.367
I went to Vegas,
and it was, like,

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:05.868
the most Vegas-y birthday ever.

00:01:05.868 --> 00:01:08.167
Me and my friends --
we flew in this cool airplane.

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:10.934
We took a party bus to
the Vegas sign.

00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:14.033
Tiesto sang me "Happy Birthday."
I gambled.

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:17.567
-Look at you and Tiesto.
I mean, I love Tiesto.

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:18.834
-He's my best friend now.

00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:21.000
-We saw Tiesto when we
first started "Late Night"

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.300
and we took all the writers.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.033
We went to a trip to
Atlantic City and saw Tiesto.

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:25.334
And it was amazing.

00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:30.000
And then I think his booth
turned into a robot and

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.834
had lasers and started
coming into the audience.

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:32.868
People were freaking out.
-No.

00:01:32.868 --> 00:01:34.000
-Yeah.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.167
-Are you sure
that really happened or...

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:38.100
-No. We were just doing --
Yeah, no, sure.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.834
-I don't know if we're allowed
to talk about that here.

00:01:39.834 --> 00:01:41.701
-You did do a lot of gambling.
-Yeah.

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:43.000
-And, so, I wanted to show this.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.267
Not to show off or anything,

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.467
but this is your
cash-out ticket in Las Vegas.

00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:50.434
Not too shabby.
Look at this right here.

00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:52.701
24 cents.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:54.033
Wow!
-Yeah.

00:01:54.033 --> 00:01:55.501
-What were you playing
and what were you losing?

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:57.868
-Slots.
And I was losing it all, baby.

00:01:57.868 --> 00:02:00.234
-Yeah, just have fun. Why not?

00:02:00.234 --> 00:02:01.367
I love this.

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:03.100
You even had birthday hats
made that says

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.434
"Zoey's 27th/21st birthday."

00:02:06.434 --> 00:02:10.801
Yeah, I love those hats.
-You're only 21 twice, so...

00:02:10.801 --> 00:02:11.868
-You could do it every year.
Come on.

00:02:11.868 --> 00:02:13.367
-Yeah, come on.

00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:15.868
-You know every time
you come on, we play games.

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:17.434
We've done bits together.
-I love bits.

00:02:17.434 --> 00:02:19.434
-Yes. You always do --
-I live for bits.

00:02:19.434 --> 00:02:22.234
-Yes. That's why we love having
you on the show.

00:02:22.234 --> 00:02:23.701
But you brought
me a game this time.

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:24.834
-I brought you a bit.

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:25.834
-Which no one
ever brings me a game.

00:02:25.834 --> 00:02:26.834
-I know.
-This is exciting.

00:02:26.834 --> 00:02:28.234
-I thought it wasn't fair.

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:30.767
So, I grew up -- My father
and my grandmother always used

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:32.701
a lot of Yiddish words
and phrases.

00:02:32.701 --> 00:02:34.000
It was a part of
the family lexicon.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.868
And I feel like you probably
know some Yiddish words.

00:02:35.868 --> 00:02:38.534
And you guys probably know some
Yiddish words, like "oy vey."

00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:40.167
Do you know "oy vey?"
-Of course, yeah.

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:41.868
-Do you know "klutz?"
-Yes.

00:02:41.868 --> 00:02:43.267
-Spiel.
-Yeah.

00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:44.701
-Okay, great.
So you know a little bit.

00:02:44.701 --> 00:02:46.734
-This is good.
-Right? We're starting off well.

00:02:46.734 --> 00:02:49.634
I picked some little bit more
advanced Yiddish words.

00:02:49.634 --> 00:02:50.634
I'm going to say the word,

00:02:50.634 --> 00:02:51.968
give you two definitions,

00:02:51.968 --> 00:02:54.601
and you're going to guess
which one it is.

00:02:54.601 --> 00:02:55.868
-Alright. I'm very competitive.
I love this.

00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:59.734
-The first one is mishpucha.

00:02:59.734 --> 00:03:00.801
-Oh, I know this.

00:03:00.801 --> 00:03:05.334
-"A," an unforeseen mishap,

00:03:05.334 --> 00:03:08.300
or, "B," family?

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.267
-Mishpucha.
-Mishpucha.

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:16.267
-And what are the two --
One is an unforeseen thing,

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:17.701
and one is family.

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:20.234
I think it's family.
"You're my mishpucha."

00:03:20.234 --> 00:03:21.334
[ Ding ]
-That's correct!

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:23.100
-Really?!
-Yeah!

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.834
-See? I know more Yiddish.
I felt like it was "family."

00:03:25.834 --> 00:03:27.200
-Yeah, the whole mishpucha.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.767
Let's bring the
whole mishpucha to Vegas.

00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:32.167
-I love Yiddish. Alright, good.

00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:34.567
-The second one -- You're gonna
be fluent in no time, guys.

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:36.100
-I know.
Look at what we're learning.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.834
-Two -- shayna punim.

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:43.033
"A," a dish containing potatoes,

00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:46.000
or "B," a pretty face?

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.200
-Punim is "face," I want to say.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.400
-You are crushing this.
[ Ding ]

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.934
-This is unbelievable?
And I did not...

00:03:52.934 --> 00:03:54.901
What was it? Shayna punim?
-Shayna punim.

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.601
Like, "Oh, what a shayna punim."
-"Oh, shayna punim"?

00:03:56.601 --> 00:03:59.334
-Yeah. Right.
-Wow.

00:03:59.334 --> 00:04:00.601
You are real good.

00:04:00.601 --> 00:04:02.234
-This is awesome.
I did not plan this out.

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:04.133
This is exciting for me.

00:04:04.133 --> 00:04:05.434
I love this. Alright.

00:04:05.434 --> 00:04:06.567
-The last one.

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:11.534
All three of these words
mean the same thing.

00:04:11.534 --> 00:04:14.133
Schmegege.

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:17.000
-I love schmegege.
-I know, schmegege.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.801
-Just saying it.
-Everyone just say it with me.

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:20.334
-Schmegege.
-Schmegege!

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:22.601
-Alright, aren't you having fun?
Schmegege?

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:24.567
-It's actually great. Schmegege.
-It's schmegege.

00:04:24.567 --> 00:04:28.367
Schmegege, schmendrick,
and schmo.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.067
-Schmendrick.
-Yeah, schmendrick.

00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:35.467
"A," these are all words for
a silly or stupid person,

00:04:35.467 --> 00:04:39.601
or, "B," these are all
affectionate terms for a baby.

00:04:39.601 --> 00:04:41.000
-It's got to be
the first one, right?

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.501
-Oh, my God.
-Look at me! Oh, my God!

00:04:42.501 --> 00:04:43.567
Am I...
♪♪

00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:47.267
[ Cheers and applause ]
Really? Respect?

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:48.701
Respect, right?
-Yeah, you earned it.

00:04:48.701 --> 00:04:51.300
-Respect right there? Come on.
Dude, that's -- Right?

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.467
Not bad?

00:04:52.467 --> 00:04:53.801
I did not --
Dude, we did not plan it.

00:04:53.801 --> 00:04:54.934
I love it.
-I have no words.

00:04:54.934 --> 00:04:56.434
-Schmegege.
-Schmegege.

00:04:56.434 --> 00:04:57.467
That's the best word
I've ever heard.

00:04:57.467 --> 00:04:59.300
-I love schmegege.
-Schmegege?

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:00.634
I mean, that's --
You guys are going to be using

00:05:00.634 --> 00:05:01.834
that every day now.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:04.701
-Now, yeah, we're
always going to say schmegege

00:05:04.701 --> 00:05:06.033
when we come out.

00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:08.367
I want to talk about your movie.
That's why you're here.

00:05:08.367 --> 00:05:11.601
This is, like -- "The Outfit."
-Yeah.

00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:15.334
-By the way, every time you come
on, you have different movies,

00:05:15.334 --> 00:05:18.467
and you have great accents
in the movie.

00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:21.567
And this is another one that you
can add to your repertoire.

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:24.067
What -- Is this,
like, a Chicago?

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:27.000
-Yeah, it's, like,
a 1950s Chicago accent.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.167
And I was so -- I really
wanted this movie.

00:05:29.167 --> 00:05:30.467
It was such a cool script.

00:05:30.467 --> 00:05:32.734
And Mark Rylance, who's one of
the greatest actors

00:05:32.734 --> 00:05:35.400
of all time -- I was really
nervous to work with him.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:37.000
And it was shooting in London.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.434
I knew I had to
prepare properly,

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:41.767
so I went to go see a dialect
coach, get the accent great.

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:45.100
I came to the first day
of rehearsal, and everybody --

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.667
Excluding Mark,
who's playing an Englishman,

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:50.767
everybody had
this distinct Chicago accent.

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:53.367
I had a
completely different accent.

00:05:53.367 --> 00:06:00.167
I showed up with, like, a really
bizarre Mid-Atlantic accent.

00:06:00.167 --> 00:06:01.968
I started talking
like this, darling.

00:06:01.968 --> 00:06:04.300
Like, the entire movie,
I'm just talking like this,

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.067
and Mark Rylance
is looking at me like,

00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:07.934
"What is this American doing?"

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:09.033
-What is going...

00:06:09.033 --> 00:06:10.634
But it's just, "Darling --"

00:06:10.634 --> 00:06:11.667
-"Darling, darling.

00:06:11.667 --> 00:06:13.133
It's so great to
see you, darling."

00:06:13.133 --> 00:06:15.534
Like, it just -- It looked like
I was a really bad imitation

00:06:15.534 --> 00:06:17.334
of an old movie.

00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:19.501
And that was mortifying,
but, eventually,

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:20.834
I got it.

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:21.934
I went to the director,
I was like, "Don't say anything.

00:06:21.934 --> 00:06:22.968
I got it.

00:06:22.968 --> 00:06:24.133
Tomorrow, it'll be Chicago,

00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:25.133
and the Mid-Atlantic
will be in the trash."

00:06:25.133 --> 00:06:26.434
It was -- Yeah.

00:06:26.434 --> 00:06:30.000
-But what different accents
can you do right now?

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.467
Can we just rattle some off?
-Oh, rattle some.

00:06:32.467 --> 00:06:33.767
-Give me --

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:35.334
-Well, I feel like,
honestly, like,

00:06:35.334 --> 00:06:37.200
the first one that comes to mind
is the accent I did

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:38.901
in "Zombieland," because, like,
it's, like,

00:06:38.901 --> 00:06:42.434
inspired by the Valley and
I'm literally from the Valley.

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:45.400
-Wow. That's perfect.
-And, like...

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:46.734
You know,
you're so good at accents,

00:06:46.734 --> 00:06:48.567
and Minnesotan is so much fun.

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:50.834
Minnesotan -- don't cha know? --
is such a fun accent to do.

00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:52.567
-Oh, Minnesota, yeah.
-Yeah?

00:06:52.567 --> 00:06:54.300
-Yeah. Love Minnesota.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:55.400
-Oh, yeah.
-Oh, yeah.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:56.968
-Oh, yeah. Of course.

00:06:56.968 --> 00:06:58.367
I'm gonna just keep
copying you now.

00:06:58.367 --> 00:06:59.434
-Oh, you are?
-Yeah, why not?

00:06:59.434 --> 00:07:00.634
-Well...
-Well...

00:07:00.634 --> 00:07:01.834
-...now...
-...now...

00:07:01.834 --> 00:07:02.968
-...I'm gonna do a...
-...I'm gonna do a...

00:07:02.968 --> 00:07:04.167
-...Southern person.
-...Southern person.

00:07:04.167 --> 00:07:05.300
-That's exactly right.
-That's exactly right.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:07.000
-And welcome to the show,
and you're just --

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.133
-Welcome to the show, now.

00:07:08.133 --> 00:07:10.033
I love Southern,
but you know what I have to say?

00:07:10.033 --> 00:07:12.667
I think my favorite accent
of all is an accent

00:07:12.667 --> 00:07:14.701
that comes from a town
not so far from here, Jimmy.

00:07:14.701 --> 00:07:16.067
-Where is that, now?

00:07:16.067 --> 00:07:19.801
-Long Island.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:19.801 --> 00:07:21.300
-You love the Long Island?

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.834
-Yeah, my grandmother,
the one who taught us all that

00:07:23.834 --> 00:07:25.767
Yiddish earlier
that you were so good at --

00:07:25.767 --> 00:07:27.067
she is from Long Island.

00:07:27.067 --> 00:07:28.734
-Oh, my God.
-She's from Long Island.

00:07:28.734 --> 00:07:30.400
She talks like this.
It's amazing.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:32.400
And if she were here,
she would tell you

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:33.567
you got a shayna punim.

00:07:33.567 --> 00:07:36.968
-[ Laughs ]
Oh, I love you and I love her.

00:07:36.968 --> 00:07:38.968
That's the next movie.
You've got to do that.

00:07:38.968 --> 00:07:39.968
-Who is going to hire me
to do that accent?

00:07:39.968 --> 00:07:41.033
-Oh, you have to do --

00:07:41.033 --> 00:07:42.200
-I sound like
I smoke a pack a day,

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:43.200
but I've never had
a cigarette in my life.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.267
-But that's perfect.

00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:45.467
You're going to
rock that, trust me.

00:07:45.467 --> 00:07:47.400
You're going to take that
and rock that in a movie.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.267
Can we tell everyone what
"The Outfit" is about?

00:07:49.267 --> 00:07:52.167
-Yes. "The Outfit"
is about an expert tailor

00:07:52.167 --> 00:07:54.868
who has to outwit a bunch
of very dangerous gangsters

00:07:54.868 --> 00:07:56.267
in order to survive a night.

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:58.734
The movie takes place
in one night

00:07:58.734 --> 00:08:00.267
on one set with six actors.

00:08:00.267 --> 00:08:01.267
It's really cool.

00:08:01.267 --> 00:08:02.801
It feels like a play.

00:08:02.801 --> 00:08:06.300
And you are constantly trying to
tell who is telling the truth.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.200
-Yeah. And it's you,
Mark Rylance. Come on.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:09.467
I want to see this.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:09.467 --> 00:08:11.267
Here's Zoey Deutch in
"The Outfit."

00:08:11.267 --> 00:08:12.467
Take a look.

00:08:12.467 --> 00:08:15.234
-I do not need you
telling me who to date.

00:08:15.234 --> 00:08:16.934
-I didn't mean it like that.

00:08:16.934 --> 00:08:18.567
-You meant it like,

00:08:18.567 --> 00:08:20.667
"I saw you smiling
at Richie Boyle" earlier,

00:08:20.667 --> 00:08:21.834
and now I'm petrified that

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:23.000
you're running with
a bad batch."

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.133
-Those men may be customers,

00:08:25.133 --> 00:08:27.601
but they are not gentlemen.

00:08:27.601 --> 00:08:30.701
-Could have fooled me in those
nice clothes you make for them.

00:08:30.701 --> 00:08:33.267
-If we only allowed angels
to be customers,

00:08:33.267 --> 00:08:35.267
then we'd have
no customers at all.

00:08:35.267 --> 00:08:38.100
-Do we let all of our customers
keep locked boxes in back?

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.467
-It was you who was
smiling at him.

00:08:40.467 --> 00:08:42.133
-I've spent my entire life

00:08:42.133 --> 00:08:44.100
around animals
like Richie Boyle.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:45.133
You want to get them
to back off,

00:08:45.133 --> 00:08:46.200
you look them dead in the eyes

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:47.434
and you pretend
you're one of them.

00:08:47.434 --> 00:08:49.467
-You don't need me
worrying after you.

00:08:49.467 --> 00:08:50.701
-That's right.

00:08:50.701 --> 00:08:54.133
-Oh! Zoey Deutch, everybody!

00:08:54.133 --> 00:08:56.801
"The Outfit"
is in theaters March 18th.

